    "perplexity": {
      "description": "The Great Ray Charles is a 1957 Atlantic album that captures Ray Charles in a strikingly different setting from the gospel-tinged R&B that made his name. Built around his piano rather than his voice, it is an all-instrumental jazz record with elegant swing, blues phrasing, and a polished small-group feel, showing Charles as a sophisticated keyboard stylist rather than just a soul singer.\n\nThe album is often valued as an important early statement of Charles’s versatility, and as one of the Atlantic-era releases that helped reintroduce him to jazz audiences. With Quincy Jones associated with the arrangements and the title track “The Ray” standing out as a highlight, it has the feel of a musician stretching beyond category, balancing warmth, virtuosity, and understated groove.",
